So for the boys, he has 7 and needs to choose 3. That should be C(7, 3). Let me calculate that. 7! divided by (3! times (7-3)! ) which is 7! / (3!4!). Let me compute that. 7*6*5 / 3*2*1 = 35. Wait, because 7! is 7×6×5×4! and the 4! cancels with the denominator's 4! So yeah, 7×6×5 divided by 6 (since 3! is 6) gives 35. So 35 ways to choose the boys.

Then for the girls, there are 4 girls and he needs to pick 2. So that's C(4, 2). Let me compute that. 4! / (2! * 2!) = (4×3×2! ) / (2! * 2) )= (4×3)/2 = 6. So 6 ways to choose the girls.

