For all x, f(-x) = f(x) (even)
and
f(-x) = -f(x) (odd).

So if both are true, then f(x) must equal -f(x) for all x. Let me see. If I set those two expressions equal to each other, since both equal f(-x), then f(x) = -f(x). Adding f(x) to both sides gives 2f(x) = 0, so f(x) = 0. 

Wait, so does this mean that the only function that is both even and odd is the zero function? Let me check that. Suppose f(x) is zero for all x. Then f(-x) is also zero. So f(-x) = f(x) = 0, which satisfies evenness. Also, f(-x) = 0 = -0 = -f(x), which satisfies oddness. So yes, the zero function works. 

But is there any other function that could satisfy both conditions? Suppose there's some x where f(x) isn't zero. Let's say f(a) ≠ 0 for some a. Then from f(a) = -f(a), we get 2f(a) = 0, which implies f(a) = 0. Contradiction. So no such function can exist except the zero function. 

